WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Welcome to this short explainer on building products that customers actually want.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Let's kick things off by meeting Michal. She's a product manager at a tech company called Velox.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:18.060
So recently, she sat down to review her team's output for the last quarter, and, well, she had a pretty painful realization about the way they were working.

4
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:23.100
Michal counted up the features her team had shipped, and the numbers were honestly kind of shocking.

5
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:29.100
While they successfully launched eight features right on time, only two are in real use by their customers today.

6
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.860
other six? Well, they were built simply because someone important asked for them, and nobody

7
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:40.290
bothered to check if actual users wanted them first. Ouch. The feature factory trap, where we

8
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:45.430
meet reality at the most expensive moment. You see, this dismal success rate isn't some weird

9
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:51.130
accident. It's actually the system working exactly as designed when teams operate merely to churn out

10
00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:56.440
code. The crucial point here is that we really have to internalize two inconvenient truths.

11
00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.620
First, at least half of our ideas, they're simply not going to work.

12
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.540
And second, even the really good ones take multiple iterations to actually deliver value.

13
00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.220
Accepting these truths means realizing that taking a massive feature list for management

14
00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:16.000
and just tossing it straight into development is basically a disguised, highly expensive waterfall process.

15
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.040
We are meeting reality way too late, usually after we've already spent the money to build the thing.

16
00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:22.970
So how do we fix this?

17
00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.310
We move to section two, the four product risks.

18
00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:32.190
which is all about testing ideas before writing code. To escape the feature factory trap, we've

19
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:36.550
got to change our habits. The absolute most dangerous moment for a product is when everyone

20
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:42.110
in the room nods and agrees an idea is great. We have to stop right there and test that idea against

21
00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:48.120
four distinct risks, using actual evidence, not just our gut feelings. To combat this danger,

22
00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:53.620
a true product trio divides and conquers these real risks. The product manager, the designer,

23
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:58.600
and the tech lead team up to answer these core questions long before production code is ever

24
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:04.480
written. Now, I want you to pay special attention to that first one, the value risk. It is absolutely

25
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:09.020
critical and honestly often the most neglected. Because finding out something works technically,

26
00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:14.400
that's relatively easy. But finding out nobody actually wants to use it, that is a whole lot more

27
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.750
painful. Rise of empowered teams, shifting from mercenaries to missionaries. Because changing

28
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:25.170
how we test risks also means completely changing how we view the brilliant people building the

29
00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:30.550
product. It's a profound shift in organizational culture. The difference here is night and day.

30
00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:36.250
An empowered team of missionaries focuses on solving a specific problem and moving a specific

31
00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:42.190
metric. They innovate because the solution is born inside the team. This is entirely unlike

32
00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:47.710
a standard feature team of mercenaries who just blindly build what they're told and measure their

33
00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:53.080
success merely by what shipped on time. Let's look at Michal again for a second. When her team was

34
00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:57.660
asked to build a Gantt chart, just because a competitor had one, they didn't just blindly

35
00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:03.300
build the CEO's requested feature. By refusing to just copy-paste a request into the backlog,

36
00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:09.160
they actually figured out the underlying problem. Managers couldn't spot team overload. So they

37
00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:14.160
tested some ideas and solved the real problem with a super simple workload alert in just two

38
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:19.850
weeks, all because nobody dictated the exact solution up front. Continuous discovery in action,

39
00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.950
building a weekly habit. So what does this transition from order taking actually look

40
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:29.940
like in a product manager's real day-to-day calendar? How do we build this habit? Well,

41
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.680
instead of writing a massive, boring requirements document, McCall presented her team with a

42
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.500
crystal clear challenge. 30% of our users don't open the dashboard at all. Why? And what are we

43
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.020
going to do about it? She kicked off a fast-paced discovery process instead of handing them a spec

44
00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:50.940
sheet. And notice how incredibly fast this moves. In just three weeks, they went from interviewing

45
00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:56.580
users, who, by the way, were totally confused by having too many metrics, to validating a prototype's

46
00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:01.820
value. They proved they were building the right thing, testing it cheaply and quickly, way before

47
00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:07.490
the developers even touched the final architecture. The tangible impact of this process? Undeniable.

48
00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:12.270
Dashboard opens literally skyrocketed from 30% to 67%.

49
00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:17.330
But honestly, the absolute most profound shift happened within the team's culture itself.

50
00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:25.120
As Velux's senior developer put it, this was the first time they felt they were solving a real problem, not just executing instructions.

51
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:26.560
Think about that.

52
00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:33.180
When you stop handing engineers a list of chores and start giving them a real problem to solve, you unlock their true potential.

53
00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.860
They become deeply invested in the outcome, not just the output.

54
00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:41.800
So as you prepare to dive into this course module, I want you to ask yourself a crucial

55
00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.700
question about your own daily work.

56
00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:48.520
Is your team just building features or are you discovering what actually deserves to

57
00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:48.840
be built?

58
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.980
Are you just executing a list or are you validating real value?

59
00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.260
Keep these core principles in mind as you read through the upcoming materials and get

60
00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:00.440
ready to learn exactly how to become a missionary for your product.

61
00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:01.940
Enjoy the rest of the stage.

